00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:16.630 align:start position:0%
 
you  I began the work that ultimately led to  the sliding filament theory of muscle  contraction in 1950 I was a research  student in a small MRC unit with John  Kendrew as my supervisor and Max Perutz  being the head of the unit and that was  it it was just three people I had been a  student in physics and my main interest  had been in experimental particle  physics but after graduation I decided  to change field completely during this  unit and was in fact in complete freedom  to choose my own particular subject of  research during the first couple of  years I spent doing some work on  hemoglobin and learning a bit of biology  of which I was totally ignorant  originally and then I chose muscle as a  possible topic because I'd found that  although a lot of luck was known about  the mechanics and energetics and  biochemistry of muscle virtually nothing  was known about its submicroscopic  structure nor about what actually  happened during contraction now one way  of getting that information might be  that if muscle was composed of regular  repeating or semi crystalline units of  these two structural proteins then one  might get evidence about what was  happening from x-ray diffraction however  people have looked at wide-angle x-ray  diffraction which showed you any changes  in the polypeptide chain configuration  and essentially if you didn't show  anything and it also looked at dried  muscle by low angle diffraction which  showed you  structural regularities and size of  protein molecules but that hadn't been  informative either  however I'd learned that one had to keep  protein crystals fully hydrated if you  were going to get anything useful in an  x-ray diagram from them so I thought  well maybe that would be true of muscle  and maybe muscle really did contain lots  of regular structures if anyone could  attack it the right way and so for that  purpose I built a miniaturized x-ray  camera which would optimize the the  sensitivity of images collected on film  and it's also very fortunate that my  supervisor John Kendrew knew a man  called banal in London who in whose  x-ray group two people were actually  building a very fine focus  high-intensity x-ray generator x-ray  tube for another purpose and they were  kind enough to give me an early  prototype of this which formed an  essential part of the x-ray setup that I  built I was very thrilled and relieved  to see that I did in fact get quite a  well-defined x-ray diagram not not very  detailed but it consisted basically of  two two reflections one here and then  another one a bit further out and the  spacing of these told one they came from  a hexagonal arrangement of objects and I  concluded that this must be an octagonal  arrangement of myosin filaments but what  was particularly interesting was that in  a muscle in Riga the pattern had the  same spacing that approximately but the  relative intensity of the two spots had  had changed and this told one that there  was some alteration in the character of  the lattice and that in fact you can  projection of the electron density from  those diagrams this is a myosin filament  here on my picture and then this is  another myosin filament there and  another one in the lattice over here in  between the myosin filaments is what I  took to be actin filaments which  appeared at low density in the relaxed  muscle but in rygg or muscle their  density was much increased now I knew  that actin and myosin tended to combine  with others in the absence of ATP that's  in a rag or muscle so I concluded and  turned out I was correct that that was  in fact what I was seeing a double array  of actin and myosin filaments  interpenetrating with each other  I could also see some reflections axial  reflections which give you information  about the longitudinal regularities  along the length of the muscle this is  spacing of about 145 angstroms and there  are another series reflections not shown  here the interesting so it showed that  the muscle was a sort of semi  crystalline structure at this scale and  these reflections didn't change in  spacing when the muscle was passively  stretched now I made a mistake at that  point I thought that these probably  these reflections probably all came from  the actin filaments so I concluded the  actin filaments didn't change in length  but I still had this picture that  perhaps the myosin filaments somehow  others shortened down anyway I finished  my PhD and went to MIT to Swift lab  which was a scent of a new center for  doing electron microscope work because I  wanted to make sure that these this  double array of filaments really existed  so by fixing embedding and section  muscle I was able to look at  cross-sections in the electron  microscope and lo and behold one could  see the double array of filaments very  very clearly this was this was a rabbit  muscle in Riga this is a myosin filament  and that that's another myosin filament  then the actin filaments are in this  little ring of six of them around each  myosin Center so that that was very  encouraging and then shortly after this  early in 1953 gene Hanson came to the  same lab to learn electric microscopy -  because she'd been studying isolated  myofibrils as seen in the light  microscope but she was a zoologist by  training and we decided we were both  doing the same thing essentially we  decided to combine our different  approaches and this worked out very  successfully one of the first  experiments we did was to look at my  fibrils in the light microscope on the  covers on a slide with the coverslip  over them in the phase contrast  microscope and you get very nice images  of the a and I bends  I'll show you one in a moment and when  we irrigated these with solutions known  to extract myosin from muscle we found  that the dense material in the a band  here was rapidly removed leaving a sort  of ghost fiber which consisted of the  original Z lines and a segment of  filament which we concluded and we're  probably acting and we later found that  applying actin extraction procedures  would remove this second set of  filaments as well these are just showing  the density plots before and after  myosin extract  so this was sort of quite a  revolutionary finding for us and but  after a few hours we'd sort of  reoriented our way of thinking about  things and we realized that we were  looking at two Oh  partially interpenetrating but  overlapping arrays of myosin filaments  and actin filaments and that the  cross-sections I'd seen had been through  the overlap region here and so that this  is what I showed before the overlap  region in the H zone which where the  actin filaments have stopped you just  see the thick filaments and in the eye  bands you can see the cross sections of  the thin filaments so we we then had a  quite new model of the structure of the  muscle and in between the two sets of  filaments were these cross bridges that  I'd have postulated must be there to  enable the two sets of the actin and  myosin filaments to interact with each  other so we published the short paper on  this in nature but at Smith's request we  emitted any speculation about the Herot  and how this system would actually work  but what we knew was that there were  these two types of these two axial  series of x-ray reflections which didn't  change during passive stretch and so we  thought well maybe they didn't change  you're in contraction either and that  would mean that the two sets of  filaments would themselves remain  constant in length and that contraction  would be achieved by the actin filaments  sliding further in to the array of  myosin filaments and when we after a lot  of hard work we were able to get  sufficient images of muscle at different  stages of contraction to prove that  point and you can see this is  a slightly stretched myofibril then we  give it a little bit of ATP and it  shortens down a little the I bands the  clearer regions become shorter that the  a bands stay constant in length and as  it shortens further still the I bands  get even shorter and ultimately that  they almost disappear if we can also  extract myosin from a stretched fibril  and we can see the large gap between the  ends of the actin filaments but when on  the course a different fibril which is  contracted we can see this gap between  the actin shortening down and then  disappearing so we can measure the  length of the I filaments and that was  constant during contraction so so this  was written up for nature and published  in late May in 1954 along with another  paper by Andrew Huxley's actually no  relation of mine and now neither gurkha  who had used special interference  microscope to look it's intact fibers  and they'd found the same thing insofar  as the a band stayed constant in length  but that their optics when such as to be  able to see they measure the length of  the actin filaments so that would so  that we thought was pretty strong  evidence for the this type of model but  it still remained quite controversial  for a number of years particularly  because I think some of the other  workers in the field hadn't thought of  got used to this combination of x-rays  in the M and Ferries microscopy but I  think it's a few more people were  probably convinced by some electron  micrographs that I was able to get two  or three years later which showed the  longer scheudle sections through the  myofibril through the muscle fiber in  which you can see the thick filaments  very clearly I think stopping at the end  of the a band whereas the thin filaments  continue on and into the I bands out  here and you can also at higher  magnification you can see these cross  bridges between the actin and myosin  filaments that I'd suppose must be able  to produce the relative sliding force  that produces contraction there's any  general conclusions about how you go  about a problem you can draw from this  well I'm not sure that there are urban  and I think I was very lucky because I  had a clearly defined problem and I was  able to find some new techniques which  provided a way into it so if you can  repeat that process you might get lucky  like I did thank you  you

